WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.968 --> 00:00:04.234
-Our first guest tonight is
an iconic, Emmy-winning actor.

00:00:04.234 --> 00:00:08.033
His memoir, "Being Henry:
The Fonz and Beyond,"

00:00:08.033 --> 00:00:09.200
is available now.

00:00:09.200 --> 00:00:10.767
Please welcome back to the show,
our friend,

00:00:10.767 --> 00:00:12.734
Henry Winkler, everybody!

00:00:12.734 --> 00:00:15.400
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:15.400 --> 00:00:21.601
♪♪

00:00:21.601 --> 00:00:27.767
♪♪

00:00:27.767 --> 00:00:33.968
♪♪

00:00:33.968 --> 00:00:35.601
Oh, Henry.

00:00:35.601 --> 00:00:37.634
-I'm so glad Connor is here,

00:00:37.634 --> 00:00:39.300
because he's the most requested.

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:42.100
I'm going out on the road,
so I just want you

00:00:42.100 --> 00:00:44.100
to keep a slot open for me.

00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:46.067
-You maybe need a touring
drummer with you?

00:00:46.067 --> 00:00:47.367
-That's all I have,
is a drummer.

00:00:47.367 --> 00:00:48.667
-Oh, gotcha.
-Yeah.

00:00:48.667 --> 00:00:51.367
-Just you, Henry on stage
with a drummer.

00:00:51.367 --> 00:00:53.534
-A joke and a ba-bum.
-Oh, you need that.

00:00:53.534 --> 00:00:54.601
-Yeah.

00:00:54.601 --> 00:00:55.767
-There you go.
That's very smart.

00:00:55.767 --> 00:00:57.400
Henry, I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:57.400 --> 00:00:59.534
Your energy
is one of my favorite

00:00:59.534 --> 00:01:00.901
in all of showbiz.

00:01:00.901 --> 00:01:03.300
-You don't have to say that.
-Well, it's a true thing.

00:01:03.300 --> 00:01:04.734
I know we can't talk
about your acting,

00:01:04.734 --> 00:01:06.934
and thank God,
because of the SAG strike.

00:01:06.934 --> 00:01:08.434
Thank God.

00:01:08.434 --> 00:01:10.067
I don't want you to think
I don't want to talk about it,

00:01:10.067 --> 00:01:12.367
because it's not exceptional.
-No, no.

00:01:12.367 --> 00:01:14.667
The strike, it should be over.
-It should.

00:01:14.667 --> 00:01:15.634
Well, I hope it ends soon.

00:01:15.634 --> 00:01:16.801
-How does a town

00:01:16.801 --> 00:01:19.934
that makes entertainment
for the entire world

00:01:19.934 --> 00:01:21.501
not come together and go,

00:01:21.501 --> 00:01:23.167
"You know what?
We can figure this out."

00:01:23.167 --> 00:01:24.934
-I know. They make dreams.

00:01:24.934 --> 00:01:27.834
[ Cheers and applause ]
Make some dreams come true.

00:01:27.834 --> 00:01:30.701
So se can't talk about
your acting.

00:01:30.701 --> 00:01:32.067
And thankfully --
-This is a new sweater.

00:01:32.067 --> 00:01:33.434
-Is it a new sweater?
-It's a new sweater.

00:01:33.434 --> 00:01:35.100
-Oh, my God.
[ Cheers ]

00:01:35.100 --> 00:01:37.167
-Now, be honest,
when you buy the sweater,

00:01:37.167 --> 00:01:38.567
do you think, "I'm going to
save it for Seth"?

00:01:38.567 --> 00:01:39.567
-Yes.
-Really?

00:01:39.567 --> 00:01:40.834
-Yes.
-You know what?

00:01:40.834 --> 00:01:41.968
I know you're a bad liar,

00:01:41.968 --> 00:01:43.200
so I'm going to take you
at your word.

00:01:43.200 --> 00:01:45.133
-Okay.
-Alright, so we can't

00:01:45.133 --> 00:01:46.234
talk about your acting,
but you did --

00:01:46.234 --> 00:01:47.434
-That character.

00:01:47.434 --> 00:01:48.834
-We can talk about
this character right here.

00:01:48.834 --> 00:01:49.834
This gentleman,
I think we know very well.

00:01:49.834 --> 00:01:51.167
-You know what I can do?
-What?

00:01:51.167 --> 00:01:53.100
-Heyyy.
-There you go.

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:54.534
You can do that,

00:01:54.534 --> 00:01:56.534
and we know
who you're talking about.

00:01:56.534 --> 00:01:58.501
And then I added a word.

00:01:58.501 --> 00:02:00.834
"Whoa."
-Yeah.

00:02:00.834 --> 00:02:01.968
-Did you, was that your idea,
was "whoa"?

00:02:01.968 --> 00:02:03.200
-It was mine.

00:02:03.200 --> 00:02:05.734
It came from my favorite sport,
horseback riding.

00:02:05.734 --> 00:02:10.267
-Oh, really?
-"Hey, Paula, whoa."

00:02:10.267 --> 00:02:11.934
-Because you were an
equestrian in your youth.

00:02:11.934 --> 00:02:14.434
-I was. Now...
-Right.

00:02:14.434 --> 00:02:15.534
-...it's impossible.
-Sure, you're too busy.

00:02:15.534 --> 00:02:17.801
-I can't even walk down stairs,

00:02:17.801 --> 00:02:20.801
let alone straddle a saddle.

00:02:20.801 --> 00:02:23.634
-I feel like a lot of
actors and actresses

00:02:23.634 --> 00:02:26.400
don't like to talk about
their past work.

00:02:26.400 --> 00:02:27.634
They want to say,
"Oh, I've moved on."

00:02:27.634 --> 00:02:29.133
-This guy --
-You love this guy.

00:02:29.133 --> 00:02:32.634
-Do you know
how loyal this guy is?

00:02:32.634 --> 00:02:36.400
He gave me notes on this guy.
-Really?

00:02:36.400 --> 00:02:41.868
-He had me cut page 57.

00:02:41.868 --> 00:02:43.234
-I mean, over the years,

00:02:43.234 --> 00:02:44.934
I assume some years
more than others,

00:02:44.934 --> 00:02:47.567
this was probably a very popular
Halloween costume.

00:02:47.567 --> 00:02:48.901
-You know what? It was.

00:02:48.901 --> 00:02:52.367
I have the original costume
still in the packaging.

00:02:52.367 --> 00:02:53.734
-Really?
-Yes.

00:02:53.734 --> 00:02:56.567
I thought that would be sort of
at a museum of some kind.

00:02:56.567 --> 00:02:58.467
-The jacket.
-Okay, the jacket.

00:02:58.467 --> 00:02:59.734
The jacket's in the museum.
You've got the rest of it.

00:02:59.734 --> 00:03:00.834
-The jacket is in the museum.

00:03:00.834 --> 00:03:02.234
-Okay, that's very good.

00:03:02.234 --> 00:03:03.634
Did you ever have a trick
or treater come to your door?

00:03:03.634 --> 00:03:04.868
-Do you know what?

00:03:04.868 --> 00:03:09.267
My grandson, Ace,

00:03:09.267 --> 00:03:12.667
my grandson,
my oldest grandson...

00:03:12.667 --> 00:03:16.200
My favorite thing
is to go to my daughter's house,

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:19.100
sit on the stoop with
with my grandsons

00:03:19.100 --> 00:03:21.868
and all of their friends
and the children that come

00:03:21.868 --> 00:03:26.267
and my granddaughters come
and give out the candy.

00:03:26.267 --> 00:03:28.501
And I walked into the house,

00:03:28.501 --> 00:03:33.367
and he was dressed with his hair
slicked back as the Fonz.

00:03:33.367 --> 00:03:34.434
[ Crowd awws ]
-Yeah?

00:03:34.434 --> 00:03:38.801
-My heart flew out of my body,

00:03:38.801 --> 00:03:40.334
honest to God.
-That's really wonderful.

00:03:40.334 --> 00:03:41.567
-Yeah.

00:03:41.567 --> 00:03:44.567
-You seem to have
a great many positive memories,

00:03:44.567 --> 00:03:47.367
not just about your work
and also about your family.

00:03:47.367 --> 00:03:49.133
When you write a memoir
like this,

00:03:49.133 --> 00:03:51.300
I imagine you also
have to do research.

00:03:51.300 --> 00:03:54.067
Were there things that you,
through writing this book,

00:03:54.067 --> 00:03:55.100
remembered that you'd forgotten?

00:03:55.100 --> 00:03:56.100
-Do you know what?

00:03:56.100 --> 00:03:58.367
I made a long list of things

00:03:58.367 --> 00:04:03.300
I forgot that I didn't remember,
you know?

00:04:03.300 --> 00:04:05.467
And I kept saying,
"Can I put them in the book?

00:04:05.467 --> 00:04:06.868
Can I put them in?"
And they said, "Well, you know,

00:04:06.868 --> 00:04:08.601
the soft cover is going
to come eventually."

00:04:08.601 --> 00:04:10.300
[ Laughter ]
-Gotcha.

00:04:10.300 --> 00:04:12.734
-That's a very polite way
for an editor to say no.

00:04:12.734 --> 00:04:14.300
-"No."
-Yeah.

00:04:14.300 --> 00:04:16.734
-Also your lovely wife, Stacey,

00:04:16.734 --> 00:04:18.667
was here tonight,
who is often here with you.

00:04:18.667 --> 00:04:20.133
-Yes.
-And I'm very happy to see her.

00:04:20.133 --> 00:04:23.701
You gave her a platform
to add in to this book.

00:04:23.701 --> 00:04:26.234
Well, I didn't give her
the platform.

00:04:26.234 --> 00:04:29.000
Her platform is hers.
-Okay. Got it.

00:04:29.000 --> 00:04:30.467
-Yeah, I think
she even built it.

00:04:30.467 --> 00:04:32.400
-Right.
-Yeah.

00:04:32.400 --> 00:04:33.501
-Some might even say
it's a pedestal.

00:04:33.501 --> 00:04:35.567
Hello? Hello?

00:04:35.567 --> 00:04:37.467
-So was this an effort

00:04:37.467 --> 00:04:40.334
to get her side
of certain memories or...?

00:04:40.334 --> 00:04:43.267
-You know, with all
the children's books

00:04:43.267 --> 00:04:45.734
that I've written,
I wrote with a partner, Lynn,

00:04:45.734 --> 00:04:47.801
and so I was comfortable
with that.

00:04:47.801 --> 00:04:50.234
And then my son, our son Max,

00:04:50.234 --> 00:04:53.567
who is a director
and a producer now,

00:04:53.567 --> 00:04:56.801
and he always said,
"You got a lot of stories, Dad.

00:04:56.801 --> 00:04:59.000
You should write a book.
You should do your memoir."

00:04:59.000 --> 00:05:00.701
And I said, "I can't do that."

00:05:00.701 --> 00:05:04.067
So Esther Newberg --
-Why did you think

00:05:04.067 --> 00:05:05.501
you couldn't do it?

00:05:05.501 --> 00:05:10.634
-Because a lot of the time,
I think I cannot do something,

00:05:10.634 --> 00:05:14.200
and then I bore myself
with the "no,"

00:05:14.200 --> 00:05:16.801
and eventually just say,
"Shut up and just try it."

00:05:16.801 --> 00:05:17.934
-Sure.

00:05:17.934 --> 00:05:19.367
-And it turns out that,
here's a book.

00:05:19.367 --> 00:05:20.501
-That's great.

00:05:20.501 --> 00:05:24.133
-I met a man
by the name of James Kaplan,

00:05:24.133 --> 00:05:27.467
and I didn't know
that it was my responsibility

00:05:27.467 --> 00:05:31.734
to fly James Kaplan
out to California twice,

00:05:31.734 --> 00:05:35.901
put him up,
and feed him three times a day.

00:05:35.901 --> 00:05:38.734
-So you're saying this man
is a con man?

00:05:38.734 --> 00:05:40.801
-I'm saying this man
is a good eater.

00:05:40.801 --> 00:05:43.300
-Okay, gotcha. That's
a very polite way of saying it.

00:05:43.300 --> 00:05:46.701
-But he is also
a wonderful collaborator

00:05:46.701 --> 00:05:48.234
and a wonderful writer.

00:05:48.234 --> 00:05:50.767
-That's great.
-And so because of him,

00:05:50.767 --> 00:05:53.601
I was able then to make it
come together.

00:05:53.601 --> 00:05:55.100
-Was the process,
you would sit with him

00:05:55.100 --> 00:05:57.534
and you would tell these stories
that you've told over the years,

00:05:57.534 --> 00:05:58.767
and then he would help
shape it into...?

00:05:58.767 --> 00:06:00.734
-Exactly right.

00:06:00.734 --> 00:06:03.200
And then I would deconstruct it,

00:06:03.200 --> 00:06:05.367
I would add, like,
I thought there was a joke

00:06:05.367 --> 00:06:07.868
at the end of a story
that was left out

00:06:07.868 --> 00:06:09.767
or that I wanted to put in,

00:06:09.767 --> 00:06:13.400
maybe a person that was left out
that I wanted to include.

00:06:13.400 --> 00:06:16.167
-I would imagine
you're the sort of person

00:06:16.167 --> 00:06:19.300
who makes sure that important
people to you and your career,

00:06:19.300 --> 00:06:22.234
you would want them
to be included.

00:06:22.234 --> 00:06:23.767
-You know what,
that is exactly right.

00:06:23.767 --> 00:06:24.901
I had that feeling.

00:06:24.901 --> 00:06:28.934
And the reason is
because without them,

00:06:28.934 --> 00:06:30.534
I would not be here.

00:06:30.534 --> 00:06:33.734
You don't get here
into this chair

00:06:33.734 --> 00:06:38.200
with the great Seth Meyers
by yourself.

00:06:38.200 --> 00:06:41.901
You get here because
people have supported you,

00:06:41.901 --> 00:06:44.868
people have helped you,

00:06:44.868 --> 00:06:49.200
people have written for you
or whatever it is.

00:06:49.200 --> 00:06:50.934
-That's really lovely.
-It's true.

00:06:50.934 --> 00:06:53.834
-I'm gonna write a book called
"I Did It by Myself."

00:06:53.834 --> 00:06:56.234
[ Laughter ]

00:06:56.234 --> 00:06:57.567
-You know what?

00:06:57.567 --> 00:06:59.801
[ Applause ]

00:06:59.801 --> 00:07:01.267
-You know that's not true.

00:07:01.267 --> 00:07:02.501
-I know it's not true,

00:07:02.501 --> 00:07:03.868
but it is a really good joke.

00:07:03.868 --> 00:07:06.067
-A really good joke.
-It's a really good joke.

00:07:06.067 --> 00:07:07.300
-I have a lot more questions
for you,

00:07:07.300 --> 00:07:09.100
because this is
not your first book.

00:07:09.100 --> 00:07:11.133
You guys, we'll be right back
with more from Henry Winkler.

